30a24589" "q-87827/selection/a155b1a3a3018d45a523f5a7bfd9cd328d1a6fa1537f2afe289f22144941f90f" "q-87827/selection/3eab48cdc83f336aea2fba1a37c9bbe73667006ce38081b896b9d0b0dcd92770" "q-87827/selection/530036713df9fc7a2facc6af9b3bb78e7fdc68fe7f7192acd3d9d2336620b366" "q-87827/selection/28fa5565f0218ad3f5271e9270a1ea88c80537dbad3bef1eb71b4f76722e95a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-87827" :question/id #uuid "6442f940-7379-5d45-a797-9e3a79792102" :question/math-academy-id "q-87827" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le 4 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-87827/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-87827"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-87831/selection/83e5e71bbb9d72a62e8017ce733683860a2b7c5ebebe4a38a4c1f244b6c70c45" :answer/id #uuid "8d593cee-0187-5464-9504-bf09ee5cb993" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operator